{"cluster_id": "C-3280", "topic": "replication claim", "target": "architecture/replication", "member_ids": ["B031-050"], "canonical_claim": "Forbidding `Ordinary` with CAS would break replicated recovery because the broken-tables path deliberately creates an `Ordinary` database to discard a table UUID.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-31-stage-b-strategic-review-fable-round3.md#ordinary"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3281", "topic": "replication claim", "target": "architecture/replication", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-184"], "canonical_claim": "Relink races GC in the shared pool (RPL-3): between the sender encoding a manifest and the receiver's `promote`, GC on the shared pool can condemn a blob that was live via the sender's ref; this is a TOCTOU inside promote in the same window as the general J1 zombie-writer/X1 reader-pin class, mitigated in practice by GC's two-phase graduation (`delete_pending` before `deleteExact`) plus copy-forward, but not provably closed for the relink promote path and has no dedicated test.", "issue_ids": ["RPL-3"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/reviews.md#L1159-L1167"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3282", "topic": "detached relink byte fallback", "target": "architecture/replication", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-185", "B002-116"], "canonical_claim": "A CA relink is advertised only for `try_zero_copy && !to_detached`, preventing byte-fetch fallback loops and ensuring detached fetches use the detached namespace. Additionally, `to_detached` relink is disabled and `FETCH PARTITION` always streams bytes (RPL-4): the receiver only advertises relink when `try_zero_copy && !to_detached`, so `ALTER TABLE ... FETCH PARTITION` (into `detached/`) and the byte-fetch fallback never relink \u2014 they stream full bytes even within the same pool, then content-address and dedup on landing. This is correct but a performance cliff, explicitly deferred in code as \"Relink-into-detached is a deferred optimization\".", "issue_ids": ["RPL-4"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/05-integration.md#3-the-datapartsexchange-story-replica-fetch-on-ca", "docs/superpowers/reports/reviews.md#L1168-L1173"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3283", "topic": "relink facade table-UUID derivation", "target": "architecture/replication", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-224", "AUDIT-M2-246"], "canonical_claim": "`DataPartsExchange.cpp:1084` calls `ContentAddressed::parseTableUuid` directly instead of going through the `IContentAddressedExchange` facade the relink design established, coupling core `MergeTree` code to a concrete CA helper's signature; the fix is to derive the table uuid inside `adoptPartFromManifest`, which already knows the receiver namespace. Additionally, Risk register #12 (Medium, confirmed): `DataPartsExchange.cpp:1084` calls `parseTableUuid` directly, bypassing the intended facade, and the `IContentAddressedExchange` seam header lives inside the feature directory, so core code compile-depends on a feature-dir header \u2014 the one break in an otherwise clean inward-only dependency direction. Suggested action: move uuid derivation into `adoptPartFromManifest`; relocate the interface header.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/00-REPORT.md#L553", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/05-integration.md#8-weak-places"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3284", "topic": "replication claim", "target": "architecture/replication", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-250"], "canonical_claim": "Risk register #16 (Low, confirmed): the `DataPartsExchange` relink payload rides manifest bytes over the legacy `part_id` cookie field, explicitly documented as \"no protocol field changes on this branch\" \u2014 fine intra-branch, but a release needs a real dedicated wire field.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/00-REPORT.md#L557"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3285", "topic": "replication claim", "target": "architecture/replication", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-371"], "canonical_claim": "`MergeTreeData::Transaction::renameParts`'s unconditional pre-Keeper-commit disk-transaction close (the R3 acked-then-lost fix) reorders durability for ALL `DiskObjectStorage`-backed MergeTree writes, not just CAS, and needs dedicated non-CA regression coverage (plain S3 + `ReplicatedMergeTree`, zero-copy) and its own standalone upstream presentation.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-20-umbrella-review-cas-vs-antalya-26.6.md#L82"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3286", "topic": "replication claim", "target": "architecture/replication", "member_ids": ["B001-012", "B051-088"], "canonical_claim": "Same-pool replica relinking transfers manifest information without blob bytes and requires matching content_addressed_pool_uuid; otherwise the receiver uses byte-fetch fallback.; The fetch-by-relink protocol uses `REPLICATION_PROTOCOL_VERSION_WITH_CA_RELINK`, `CA_POOL_UUID_PARAM`, and `CA_RELINK_COOKIE`; matching `pool_uuid` values let a same-pool fetch publish a local ref over shared blobs without sending bytes, while non-CA fetches retain their existing behavior.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/upstream-patch-inventory.md#data-parts-exchange", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/00-REPORT.md#L332-L338"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3287", "topic": "replication claim", "target": "architecture/replication", "member_ids": ["B001-020", "B038-095"], "canonical_claim": "CAS is enabled per disk with metadata_type = content_addressed, and zero-copy replication remains available but is mutually exclusive with CAS per disk.; For CA disks, `supportZeroCopyReplication()==false` while `supportsHardLinks()==true`; unsupported operations are rejected by independent gates, while mutations use whole-part transactions.", "issue_ids": ["B31"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/01-architecture.md#L12-L23", "docs/superpowers/cas/01-architecture.md#L507-L515", "docs/superpowers/cas/ROADMAP.md#area-operability"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3288", "topic": "replication claim", "target": "architecture/replication", "member_ids": ["B001-023", "B035-084"], "canonical_claim": "A stable minted pool_uuid in _pool_meta is the same-pool criterion for fetch-by-relink; endpoint and prefix string matching is rejected because it can produce false-positive relinks and dangling refs.; Same-pool fetch-by-relink detection must use a stable minted `pool_uuid` stored in `_pool_meta`, not endpoint-and-prefix string matching, because aliases can cause false negatives and shared proxies can cause false positives that relink to absent blobs.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/01-architecture.md#L406-L424", "docs/superpowers/cas/CONSOLIDATION-COVERAGE.md#second-pass-gap-fill", "docs/superpowers/cas/CONSOLIDATION-COVERAGE.md#second-pass-smaller-gaps"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3289", "topic": "replication claim", "target": "architecture/replication", "member_ids": ["B002-114", "B033-045", "B033-054", "B046-050"], "canonical_claim": "`REPLICATION_PROTOCOL_VERSION_WITH_CA_RELINK` is protocol version 10; mixed-version clusters negotiate the minimum version and automatically fall back to byte transfer.; The replication part-fetch HTTP names are `cas_pool_uuid`, `cas_relink`, `cas_confirm`, `cas_source_token`, and `cas_confirm_answer`, and mixed-build clusters must be fully rebuilt because version skew silently falls back to non-CAS full copies. Additionally, The replication wire-protocol literals are cas_pool_uuid, cas_relink, cas_confirm, cas_source_token, and cas_confirm_answer; both protocol sides are in DataPartsExchange.cpp, so mixed-build clusters fall back to full copies. Additionally, The naming plan changes the five part-fetch protocol literals in `DataPartsExchange.cpp` atomically within one build and updates integration-test assertions to match them.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": [".superpowers/sdd/2026-08-03-cas-naming/t3-report.md#L56-L67", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-03-cas-naming-unification.md#task-3-system-tables-server-settings-config-metadatatype", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/05-integration.md#3-the-datapartsexchange-story-replica-fetch-on-ca", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-03-cas-naming-unification-design.md#scope-what-is-renamed"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3290", "topic": "replication claim", "target": "architecture/replication", "member_ids": ["B002-115"], "canonical_claim": "The CA relink wire contract sends `pool_uuid` and encoded `PartManifest` bytes in the legacy `part_id` cookie as `part_manifest_v1`; sender identity fields in the body are non-authoritative.", "issue_ids": ["B7"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/05-integration.md#3-the-datapartsexchange-story-replica-fetch-on-ca"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3291", "topic": "replication claim", "target": "architecture/replication", "member_ids": ["B002-117", "B038-028"], "canonical_claim": "Same-pool CA replication transfers a manifest instead of blob bytes; the receiver adopts shared-pool blobs into a fresh receiver-local manifest and publishes its own ref.; `ContentAddressedExchange` replication transfers manifest bytes and lets the receiver adopt listed blobs by hash without payload transfer; decode or promote failure falls back to ordinary byte fetch.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/05-integration.md#3-the-datapartsexchange-story-replica-fetch-on-ca", "src/Disks/DiskObjectStorage/MetadataStorages/ContentAddressed/README.md#lifecycle-walkthroughs"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3292", "topic": "replication claim", "target": "architecture/replication", "member_ids": ["B002-118"], "canonical_claim": "If CA manifest adoption fails because a referenced blob is missing or condemned, the receiver publishes nothing and retries the fetch with `try_zero_copy=false`.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/05-integration.md#3-the-datapartsexchange-story-replica-fetch-on-ca"]}
