{"cluster_id": "C-3294", "topic": "replication claim", "target": "architecture/replication", "member_ids": ["B003-130"], "canonical_claim": "Within one pool, `BACKUP` is a per-disk snapshot, consolidation makes a selected snapshot pool-complete, mirror copies snapshots to a backup pool, fetch selectively pulls closures, and `RESTORE` relinks refs locally.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/10-backups.md#four-verbs"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3295", "topic": "replication claim", "target": "architecture/replication", "member_ids": ["B003-135"], "canonical_claim": "The mirror daemon runs on the backup side, reads production with read-only credentials, writes with backup credentials, lists only `cas/refs/`, copies missing snapshot-closure objects, and never propagates deletions.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/10-backups.md#mirror-daemon"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3296", "topic": "replication claim", "target": "architecture/replication", "member_ids": ["B003-138"], "canonical_claim": "No restore path writes to the backup pool; selective fetch copies a chosen snapshot closure into a fresh or living pool and local `RESTORE` republishes refs with zero data bytes.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/10-backups.md#recovery"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3297", "topic": "replication claim", "target": "architecture/replication", "member_ids": ["B003-140"], "canonical_claim": "The backup invariants are `BAK-RO` for no restore writes to backup, `BAK-EDGE-DRIVEN-MANIFESTS` for edge-only reclamation, `BAK-THIN-AFTER-MIRROR` for ordering, `BAK-INDEPENDENT-RETENTION` for destination retention, and `BAK-NO-IDENTITY-COPY` for identity objects.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/10-backups.md#backup-invariants"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3298", "topic": "relink refusal and byte-fallback policy", "target": "architecture/replication", "member_ids": ["B004-065", "B024-056", "B025-026", "B025-027", "B030-016", "B030-087", "B034-126"], "canonical_claim": "Relink promotion is authorized only by literal `yes`; missing, unknown, timeout, and transport-error outcomes are locally generated `NETWORK_ERROR` retry outcomes rather than byte-fetch success. Additionally, The typed relink boundary treats confirm `no`, `unknown`, and transport failure as `SourceProofFailed`: it aborts and throws a locally generated retry-later error rather than returning `false` for same-sender byte fallback. Additionally, The confirm wire is binary and fail-closed: only `yes` authorizes, while `No`, `Unknown`, and an absent cookie are all treated as unproven; old receivers ignore the unknown cookie and old senders offer relink without a token. Additionally, `prepareAdoptFromManifest` returns `Prepared` or `MechanismFallbackAllowed`; a confirm failure is not mechanism fallback, so the receiver must abort and throw a retry-later `NETWORK_ERROR` rather than byte-fetching on an unproven source. Additionally, In receiver state S2, an unresolved promotion must retry later and never fetch bytes, because a byte fetch could publish a part twice over a relink that may already have committed. Additionally, A relink refusal throws `NETWORK_ERROR`, is stored for retry-later with backoff and reselection, and must not issue a byte re-request to the same source; the path therefore cannot duplicate bytes or double-publish.; A relink refusal is returned as retry-later `NETWORK_ERROR`; the replication queue requeues and reselects the fetch, and the receiver must not re-request bytes from the same source because that would be unsound.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/11-walkthrough.md#relink-gates", "docs/superpowers/cas/2026-07-29-relink-confirm-per-ref-draft.md#measured-problem", "docs/superpowers/cas/BACKLOG.md#relink-confirm-busy-lane", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-24-cas-publish-confirm-and-ref-lane-safety.md#L1010-L1068", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-24-cas-publish-confirm-and-ref-lane-safety.md#L1069-L1134", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-29-cas-relink-seam-tla-gate.md#task-3-re-derivation-against-the-new-rule-set-and-the-four-state-receiver", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-23-cas-fetch-handoff-publish-confirm-design.md#failure-taxonomy"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3299", "topic": "replication claim", "target": "architecture/replication", "member_ids": ["B006-003"], "canonical_claim": "`mutateShard` is synchronous for its caller, so every queued mutation is a blocked thread waiting on a promise and the total queued items across all shards is bounded by the number of concurrently writing threads.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-03-cas-shard-mutation-queue.md#L24-L31"]}
