{"cluster_id": "C-3303", "topic": "replication claim", "target": "architecture/replication", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-204", "B015-073"], "canonical_claim": "Local-source relinks hold the source as a live `DataPartPtr`, so the source replica's edge remains pinned through the destination commit and the exposure is structurally unreachable for those relinks.; RPL-3 disposition (relink races GC in the shared pool): judged safe in practice because while the sender is transmitting the part and has not received an acknowledgment from the receiver, the sender cannot remove the part; the receiver gives an acknowledgment only after a successful write.", "issue_ids": ["#42", "RPL-3"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-14-cas-adoptevidence-relink-lifecycle-exposure.md#sub-q1-enumerate-createhardlinkadoptevidence-callers-and-whether-the-source-is-pinned", "docs/superpowers/reports/reviews.md#L1270"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3304", "topic": "replication claim", "target": "architecture/replication", "member_ids": ["B015-074"], "canonical_claim": "CAS refs are per-replica while blobs are pool-wide, so a blob's in-degree counts one edge for each replica manifest that names it.", "issue_ids": ["#42"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-14-cas-adoptevidence-relink-lifecycle-exposure.md#sub-q1-enumerate-createhardlinkadoptevidence-callers-and-whether-the-source-is-pinned"]}
